A sensor-range lookup routes through `sensor_id % NUM_SHARDS`, so the application knows the one file holding that sensor. A day-range lacks that shard-key predicate, so it must fan out to all files and combine results. Each `(sensor_id, recorded_at)` index improves the routed lookup inside an individual shard, but does not tell SQLite which shard holds a time range. Changing the shard count changes modulo results and therefore relocates existing sensor IDs. The application remains responsible for shard mapping, query routing/fan-out, result aggregation, rebalancing/migration, distributed transactions, replication, failure handling, and cross-shard consistency.

