Finally, compare the forced owner and administrator behavior:

```sql
SET ROLE rls_lab_owner;
SELECT count(*) AS owner_rows_with_force_rls
FROM rls_lab.projects;
-- 0: FORCE applies RLS; no owner policy means default deny
RESET ROLE;

SELECT count(*) AS administrator_rows
FROM rls_lab.projects;
-- 2 when the session role is a superuser
```

The application must execute `BEGIN`, set the authenticated tenant with `set_config(..., true)`, run all request queries on that same pooled connection, and then commit or roll back. Transaction-local context disappears before the connection is reused; with GUI-client auto-commit, it also disappears immediately after the setting statement, so the test needs an explicit transaction. RLS protects against forgotten tenant predicates and accidental cross-tenant access, but the application role can change a custom setting, so the backend must derive the value from verified authentication. It is not a defense against successful SQL injection or a fully compromised application role.
